      "content": "The concept of \"social friction\" was often rooted in anxieties about preserving perceived cultural purity and traditional hierarchies. Proponents frequently relied on tropes suggesting that differing racial groups possessed fundamentally incompatible social mores and values. The argument often posited that increased interaction would inevitably lead to cultural contamination or the erosion of the dominant group's established standards of living. They framed integration not as a political difference, but as a dangerous biological or cultural mixing that would degrade social capital.",
